# The local Frobenius acts on `ζ_n` by `ζ ↦ ζ ^ q`

For a number field `K`, a finite place `𝔭` with completion `κ = K_𝔭`, residue field cardinality
`q = residueCard K 𝔭`, and `n` prime to the residue characteristic `p`, the cyclotomic extension
`κ⟮ζ⟯` generated by a primitive `n`-th root of unity is *unramified* of degree `ord_n(q)`, and its
Frobenius — the unique `κ`-automorphism of `κ⟮ζ⟯` acting on the residue field by `x ↦ x ^ q` —
sends `ζ` to `ζ ^ q`:

  `∃ σ : Gal(κ⟮ζ⟯ / κ), σ ζ = ζ ^ q`.

This is the normalisation of the Frobenius of NSW (7.5.6) in the case of the cyclotomic
extension, and is the "action on the prime-to-`p` roots of unity" half of
`NK.LocalAnabelian.IsFrobeniusFor`.

The proof goes through the *global* cyclotomic field `L = K(ζ_n)`: a Frobenius `σ` at a prime
`Q ∣ 𝔭` of `𝓞 L` induces an element `σL` of `Gal(L_Q/κ)` (`decompositionToLocalGalois`) with
`σL ζ' = ζ' ^ a` for `ζ'` the image of `ζ_n` in `L_Q`, and reducing modulo `Q` shows
`a ≡ q (mod n)`.  Hence on the local cyclotomic subfield `F = κ⟮ζ'⟯` the Frobenius acts by
`ζ' ↦ ζ' ^ q`.  Since `κ⟮ζ⟯` and `κ⟮ζ'⟯` are both cyclotomic extensions of `κ` of level `n`, they
are isomorphic, and conjugating the Frobenius of `F` by such an isomorphism produces the required
automorphism of `κ⟮ζ⟯` — the action on any primitive `n`-th root of unity is transported exactly
because `ζ ↦ ζ ^ q` commutes with the identification of the two cyclotomic fields.
-/
import Mathlib
import Submission.NK.TameStructureDeep.Degree.galoisGroup_adjoin_primitiveRoot_mulEquiv
import Submission.NK.Mine.ResidueCharAutomatic
import Submission.NK.LocalStructure.Tame.residueCard_eq_pow_of_charP
